    <body>
      <sec id="article-129476.s1" sec-type="Continuing Education Activity">
        <title>Continuing Education Activity</title>
        <p>Fetal ultrasonography serves as a cornerstone in assessing fetal development, detecting anomalies, and monitoring well-being across all trimesters, with increasing importance in high-risk pregnancies complicated by obesity, hypertension, and gestational diabetes. This course reviews ultrasound examinations, which are standardized by major professional societies and are categorized as standard, specialized, or limited, each with trimester-specific indications and protocols.&#x000a0;Advances in imaging now enable earlier detection of structural anomalies, improving opportunities for timely diagnosis, genetic counseling, and intervention. Participants will also gain an understanding of the variability in terminology and application, which contributes to clinical confusion and missed diagnostic opportunities.&#x000a0;This activity for healthcare professionals is designed to enhance learners' competence in ultrasound classifications, indications, and trimester-based protocols, thereby improving&#x000a0;their ability to select appropriate studies, interpret findings accurately, and implement an appropriate interprofessional approach when employing this modality to&#x000a0;optimize patient outcomes through early identification of fetal and placental abnormalities.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Objectives:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Identify trimester-specific indications for fetal ultrasonography based on established society guidelines.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Differentiate between standard and detailed fetal anatomy examinations using imaging criteria to guide appropriate study selection.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Interpret key ultrasound findings to detect early fetal abnormalities.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Collaborate with interprofessional team members to improve care coordination and outcomes in patients undergoing fetal ultrasound imaging.</p></list-item></list>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=129476&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=129476">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-129476.s2" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>Fetal ultrasonography is an essential element in the evaluation of anomalies and fetal well-being throughout pregnancy. The increasing incidence of morbid obesity, hypertension, and gestational diabetes within the reproductive age group places this high-risk population at increased adverse fetal events, eg, stillbirth and fetal anomalies. In every trimester, specific maternal and fetal indications require additional fetal screening and assessment.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129476.r1">[1]</xref></p>
        <p>Each fetal scan has been standardized to include the minimal imaging criteria and components required by the society guidelines of the American College of Radiology (ACR), Society of Radiologists in Ultrasound (SRU), American College of Obstetrics and Gynecology (ACOG), and American Institute of Ultrasound in Medicine (AIUM), in support of Society of Maternal-Fetal Medicine (SMFM). Although ultrasound components are standardized, ultrasound terminology often differs across societies and in Current Procedural Terminology (CPT) descriptions, leading to a long history of misunderstandings among clinicians and missed opportunities for fetal assessment.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129476.r2">[2]</xref>&#x000a0;The aforementioned societies agree on classifying fetal ultrasounds as either standard, specialized, or limited examinations to mitigate ongoing confusion. The standard, specialized, and limited ultrasound criteria can be applied to each trimester. Each examination has specific trimester-based indications that must be met before performing the exam.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129476.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129476.r1">[1]</xref></p>
        <p>The standard&#x000a0;second-trimester scan, also known as a &#x0201c;level I,&#x0201d; is a simple fetal anatomic survey examination performed in low-risk pregnancies. The specialized second-trimester anatomy ultrasound, also known as &#x0201c;level II,&#x0201d; &#x0201c;detailed,&#x0201d; or &#x0201c;targeted&#x0201d; examination, is performed when abnormalities are suspected on the standard anatomy examination or in pregnancies at high risk for anatomic abnormalities. Other types of specialized examinations include a fetal echocardiogram, fetal doppler ultrasound, cervical length measurement, and biophysical profile. Finally, a limited ultrasound is performed in any trimester to answer a specific clinical question and aid in diagnosis and treatment. A limited ultrasound is typically performed in an acute setting when time is limited or as a follow-up to assess specific fetal components.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129476.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129476.r1">[1]</xref></p>
        <p>Fetal ultrasonography is a standard and routine modality performed in every pregnancy. However, the differences in approach, assessment protocols, timing, and frequency of the examinations are individualized based on the gestational age and clinical scenario. The goal of more frequent examinations in the high-risk population is to detect abnormalities at an early stage, when further intervention may still be feasible. Traditionally, a comprehensive fetal anatomic survey has been performed in the mid-second trimester to assess anatomic anomalies. Owing to improvement and advancement in ultrasonography, abnormalities can now be detected in the late first trimester. Early identification of fetal anomalies allows the patient to pursue diagnostic testing, genetic counseling, and adequate time to make an informed decision.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129476.r3">[3]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129476.r4">[4]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129476.r5">[5]</xref></p>
        <p>Given the broad nature of this topic, the purpose of this activity is to review the indications and protocols for a standard versus specialized or &#x0201c;detailed&#x0201d; fetal ultrasound by each trimester. By recognizing the indications and understanding the differences among the fetal components assessed, clinicians can correctly identify the optimal examination by gestational age for each patient and improve clinical outcomes.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-129476.s3" sec-type="Anatomy and Physiology">
        <title>Anatomy and Physiology</title>
        <p>
<bold>Fetal Assessment</bold>
</p>
        <p>The gestational sac is the first sign of a developing pregnancy, usually identifiable at approximately 5 weeks of gestation. The sac is a round fluid collection that, at times, is surrounded by 2 echogenic rings representing the layers of the decidua, also known as the double decidual sac sign. When the gestational sac is located on one side of the endometrium and surrounded by an echogenic area, this is known as the intradecidual sign. The absence of these signs does not rule out an intrauterine pregnancy. Visualization of any round or oval fluid collection within the mid-uterus likely represents an intrauterine pregnancy. However, a gestational sac must be distinguished from a &#x0201c;pseudogestational sac&#x0201d; in cases of a suspected ectopic pregnancy. The fluid from the ectopic pregnancy collects in the uterus and mimics a gestational sac. These collections usually contain irregular borders or debris.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129476.r6">[6]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129476.r7">[7]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129476.r8">[8]</xref></p>
        <p>The yolk sac is a thin-walled circular structure that develops within the gestational sac, usually after 5.5 weeks of gestation, and usually resolves by 12 weeks. The yolk sac provides nutrition and gas exchange to the developing embryo. The embryo, typically visible at approximately 6 weeks of gestation, is connected to the yolk sac at the midgut via the yolk stalk. The embryo&#x02019;s first distinguishable structure is the flickering cardiac activity that should develop by a crown-rump length of 7 mm in a normal pregnancy. The presence of a gestational sac with a yolk sac or an embryo is required for a definitive diagnosis of pregnancy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129476.r6">[6]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129476.r9">[9]</xref></p>
        <p>By 11 weeks of gestation, organogenesis is complete, and most fetal anomalies have already formed by this period. Fetal anomalies occur in 2% to 3% of pregnancies, and traditionally, a comprehensive fetal anatomic survey has been performed in the mid-second trimester to assess them. Due to advances in ultrasonography, these anomalies can now be detected in the late first trimester. The common malformations of neural tube defects (anencephaly, myelomeningocele), cystic hygromas, abdominal wall defects, limb abnormalities, and holoprosencephaly can be seen 50% of the time in singleton pregnancies at this early gestational period.</p>
        <p>Additionally, markers of aneuploidy, eg, hypoplastic nasal bone, abnormal cardiac findings, and abnormal ductus venosus flow can be seen. However, brain structures (cavum septum pellucidum or corpus callosum) or diaphragmatic hernias are not fully developed at this stage and must be evaluated in the mid-second trimester. Noting that the early appearance of developing organ structures can be mistaken as anomalies to the untrained eye is important. For example, before 13 weeks, a physiological bowel herniation into the base of the umbilical cord occurs and can be mistaken for an abdominal wall defect if specialized training is not completed before the interpretation of a late first-trimester anatomy scan.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129476.r3">[3]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129476.r4">[4]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129476.r6">[6]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129476.r5">[5]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129476.r10">[10]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Placental Evaluation</bold>
</p>
        <p>In addition to a comprehensive fetal anatomic evaluation, the placenta is evaluated for location, texture, and cord insertion. If placenta accreta spectrum (PAS) is suspected or a low-lying placenta is visualized on a transabdominal exam, a transvaginal approach for evaluating the placenta is recommended. Suspicion for PAS is based on clinical risk factors, eg, previous cesarean deliveries, in addition to ultrasound findings. In the first trimester, low implantation of the gestational sac could represent cesarean scar implantation and increase the risk of PAS.</p>
        <p>In the second and third trimesters, the presence of placenta previa, along with placental findings, eg, lacunae, disruption of the retroplacental hypoechoic zone, myometrial thinning, or uterovesical hypervascularity, greatly increases the likelihood of PAS. The presence of placenta previa with suspicion for PAS markedly increases maternal and fetal mortality, and cesarean delivery is recommended. Detection enables early interprofessional planning for cesarean delivery at a tertiary care center where experienced, specialized surgeons are available.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129476.r11">[11]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129476.r12">[12]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Maternal Pelvic Anatomy Assessment</bold>
</p>
        <p>Lastly, maternal pelvic anatomy (uterus, cervix, ovaries, adnexa) is assessed. Evaluation for abnormalities such as fibroids, adnexal masses, and Mullerian duct anomalies is performed during first-trimester and mid-second-trimester scans. The presence of ultrasound abnormalities or clinical indications may warrant additional specialized ultrasound examinations, eg, serial transvaginal cervical length measurements or limited follow-up imaging.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129476.r1">[1]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-129476.s4" sec-type="Indications">
        <title>Indications</title>
        <p>
<bold>Standard First-Trimester Ultrasound Examination</bold>
</p>
        <p>As outlined by the AIUM-ACR-ACOG-SMFM-SRU practice parameters, indications include confirmation of singleton/multiple gestation intrauterine pregnancy and chorionicity/amnionicity if applicable, confirmation of cardiac activity, estimation of gestational age, evaluation of suspected ectopic pregnancy, measurement of nuchal translucency (NT) for aneuploidy screening, vaginal bleeding, pelvic pain, suspected gestational trophoblastic disease, or maternal pelvic organ assessment.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129476.r1">[1]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Detailed First-Trimester Anatomy Ultrasound Examination (Early Comprehensive Fetal Anatomy Ultrasound)</bold>
</p>
        <p>This specialized diagnostic examination is an indication-driven examination for women at increased risk of fetal and placental anomalies performed between 12 weeks and 0 days to 13 weeks and 6 days either transabdominally or combined with the transvaginal approach. Indications include a previous child with an anomaly, increased nuchal translucency greater than 3 mm, a suspected fetal anomaly in the current pregnancy, advanced maternal age, pregestational diabetes mellitus, maternal obesity, IVF pregnancy, teratogen exposure, congenital infection exposures, multifetal gestation, positive maternal serum screening tests, and abnormal placental implantation. The best time to perform an early anatomy scan is after 13 weeks. With great emphasis, this ultrasound does not replace a detailed second-trimester anatomic survey, which should always be performed.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129476.r4">[4]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129476.r13">[13]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Standard Second-Trimester Anatomy Ultrasound Examination</bold>
</p>
        <p>This routine anatomy ultrasound is performed between 18&#x000a0;and 22 weeks in every pregnancy to assess fetal anatomy, screen for anomalies, evaluate placental location and maternal pelvic anatomy, and estimate gestational age (if not performed earlier). The standard anatomical ultrasound is performed in low-risk pregnancies who otherwise do not have indications for a detailed anatomy scan, as outlined below (see <bold>Image</bold>. Components of Standard and Detailed Examinations).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129476.r1">[1]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Detailed Second Trimester Anatomy Ultrasound Examination</bold>
</p>
        <p>According to the AIUM-ACR-ACOG-SMFM-SRU practice parameters, this scan is an indication-driven examination and is not intended to be performed in all pregnancies (see <bold>Image</bold>. Differences in Maternal and Fetal Biometry Components on Ultrasound). The indications include a previous child with an anomaly, increased nuchal translucency greater than3 mm, a suspected fetal anomaly in current pregnancy, advanced maternal age, pregestational diabetes mellitus, early gestational diabetes before 24 weeks, maternal obesity, IVF pregnancy, teratogen exposure, congenital infection exposures, multifetal gestation, positive maternal serum screening tests, abnormal placental implantation, parental chromosomal/genetic carrier, aneuploidy marker on previous ultrasound, alloimmunization, oligohydramnios, polyhydramnios, maternal drug use.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129476.r14">[14]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Specialized Third Trimester Ultrasound Examination</bold>
</p>
        <p>Third-trimester ultrasounds are indication-driven examinations. Examples of specialized ultrasound types include the biophysical profile, additional biometric measurements and follow-ups (every 3 to 4 weeks), and Doppler ultrasounds in the setting of growth restriction. Indications include fetal growth restriction or suspected macrosomia, multifetal gestation, twin-twin transfusion syndrome screening (every 2 weeks), a significant discrepancy between the uterine size and clinical dates, evaluation of fetal well-being, evaluation of prelabour rupture of membranes, preterm labor, vaginal bleeding, suspected placental abruption, follow-up evaluation for placental appearance for suspected placenta previa, vasa previa, PAS, suspected fetal death, suspected amniotic fluid abnormalities.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129476.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129476.r15">[15]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129476.r16">[16]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-129476.s5" sec-type="Contraindications">
        <title>Contraindications</title>
        <p>The absolute contraindication to performing fetal ultrasonography is patient refusal. Ultrasonography is generally safe during pregnancy and should only be used when indicated with the physician&#x02019;s order under the ALARA (as low as reasonably achievable) principle. M-mode imaging should be used to document cardiac activity, and pulse Doppler is discouraged if it will not add valuable information to the examination.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129476.r4">[4]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129476.r17">[17]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-129476.s6" sec-type="Equipment">
        <title>Equipment</title>
        <p>Most examinations are performed with gray-scale real-time scanners using a transabdominal or transvaginal approach. The approach is influenced by gestational age, maternal habitus, and fetal positioning. An abdominal curvilinear transducer with 3 to 5 MHz provides sufficient penetration for adequate resolution. Additionally, high-frequency probes (9-12 MHz) are used in the transvaginal approach.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129476.r4">[4]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129476.r18">[18]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-129476.s7" sec-type="Personnel">
        <title>Personnel</title>
        <p>According to the AIUM's accreditation policies, sonographers are required to be credentialed in their practicing specialty. Ultrasound examinations are supervised by a licensed clinician who meets the training specialty guidelines, and a qualified physician should be readily available to interpret the ultrasound examination.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129476.r3">[3]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129476.r5">[5]</xref></p>
        <p>Additionally, according to "Training Guidelines for Physicians Who Evaluate and Interpret Diagnostic Obstetric Ultrasound Examinations," physicians and sonographers who read and perform early comprehensive fetal-anatomy ultrasounds should undergo specialized training at a teaching facility that provides adequate experience to become proficient. Early comprehensive fetal anatomy ultrasound should be performed at referral centers with specialized expertise in interpreting findings and counseling patients.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129476.r3">[3]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129476.r5">[5]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-129476.s8" sec-type="Preparation">
        <title>Preparation</title>
        <p>Before starting any ultrasound examination, the sonographer should confirm the correct patient, the scan to be performed, and the reason for the scan. For transabdominal ultrasounds, the patient should ideally have a full bladder to enhance the resolution of surrounding structures. For a transvaginal approach, the bladder should be empty for optimal&#x000a0;visualization.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129476.r18">[18]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-129476.s9" sec-type="Technique or Treatment">
        <title>Technique or Treatment</title>
        <p>The detailed first-trimester anatomy ultrasound examination is a relatively new recommendation in the AIUM-ACR-ACOG-SMFM-SRU practice parameter and has yet to be adopted as the standard of care for those who meet the indications. Techniques and imaging parameters will be discussed in this section.&#x000a0;A combined transabdominal and transvaginal approach may be needed to fully visualize all structures.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>
<bold>Fetal Biometry</bold>
</p>
        <p>The techniques for assessing fetal biometry via crown-rump length, biparietal diameter (BPD), and head circumference (HC) are standard and unchanged from the AIUM-ACR-ACOG-SMFM practice parameters.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129476.r13">[13]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129476.r19">[19]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Fetal Head</bold>
</p>
        <p>Sagittal and axial planes are used to evaluate fetal head structures (see <bold>Image</bold>. Fetal Cerebellum and Cisterna Magna). The skull should be oval with calvarial ossification and no bulges. The choroid plexus should be visible on each side of the falx cerebri in the transventricular plane. In the transthalamic plane, the thalami, cerebral peduncles, third ventricle, and aqueduct of Sylvius are visualized. Angling toward the posterior fossa in the axial plane allows for visualization of the fourth ventricle. The thalami, midbrain, brain stem, fourth ventricle, and foramen magnum should also be visualized in this plane.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129476.r13">[13]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129476.r20">[20]</xref></p>
        <p>Several potential abnormalities may be identified. Within the midsagittal view, signs of early spina bifida are seen if the future cisterna magna appears abnormal or the fourth ventricle is absent or compressed. Posterior displacement of the aqueduct of Sylvius is also a marker for spina bifida. Enlargement of the fourth ventricle can be associated with the Dandy-Walker continuum. Anencephaly-acrania sequence, alobar holoprosencephaly, and large cephaloceles can also be detected during this period.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129476.r13">[13]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129476.r20">[20]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129476.r21">[21]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129476.r22">[22]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129476.r23">[23]</xref>&#x000a0;Limitations of this evaluation are that the cavum septum pellucidum and corpus callosum are not visible in the late first trimester.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129476.r13">[13]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Face and Profile</bold>
</p>
        <p>The forehead, nasal bridge, nasal bone, maxilla, and mandible should be seen in the midsagittal and coronal views. The forehead should not be protruding or flattened, and there should be a contiguous nose to upper lip contour without maxillary protuberance or gap. The retronasal triangle can identify the nasal bones, premaxillary processes, primary palate, and mandible. The coronal plane is used to visualize the orbits, lenses, and the integrity of the upper lip.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129476.r13">[13]</xref></p>
        <p>Potential abnormalities that may be identified include a maxillary gap, which is suspicious for a cleft palate, and the absence of the mandibular gap, which is suspicious for micrognathia.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129476.r24">[24]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129476.r25">[25]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Neck</bold>
</p>
        <p>In an axial or coronal view, the neck should be evaluated for abnormal fluid collections, cystic hygroma, or dilated jugular lymphatic sacs. The midsagittal plane is used to evaluate the nuchal translucency. Precise measurement is only required if the NT appears enlarged or is a part of a screening for aneuploidy. Established guidelines are used to measure nuchal translucency to reduce false-negative and false-positive results. The AIUM-ACR-ACOG-SMFM-SRU Practice Parameter outlines guidelines for NT measurement.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129476.r26">[26]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Thorax and Heart</bold>
</p>
        <p>The required structures in the axial view are cardiac position and axis; 4-chamber view without and with color Doppler; 3-vessel and trachea views with color Doppler; symmetric lungs; and diaphragm demarcation (see <bold>Image</bold>. Four Chamber View). In the coronal and sagittal views, diaphragm demarcation should also be visualized. Although color Doppler should be limited during the first trimester, color flow is essential for visualizing cardiac structures at this gestational age. The output display standard should be monitored to ensure the thermal index for bone remains below 0.7.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129476.r13">[13]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129476.r18">[18]</xref></p>
        <p>In the axial view, the lungs should be symmetric, and the ribs should appear of normal ossification and length. The heart should be one-third the size of the chest. The 4-chamber view with color Doppler should show equal-sized ventricles, with a defined ventricular septum, atria, and demonstration of mitral and tricuspid flow. The 3-vessel and trachea view with color Doppler should show the transverse aortic arch/isthmus merging with the pulmonary trunk/ductus arteriosus. There should be an antegrade flow to the left side of the trachea. In patients with suspected cardiac abnormalities, longitudinal aortic and ductal arches may be evaluated with pulsed-wave Doppler flow.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129476.r13">[13]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129476.r27">[27]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129476.r28">[28]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Abdomen and Pelvis</bold>
</p>
        <p>The axial plane at the levels of the stomach, kidneys, and bladder is evaluated (see <bold>Image</bold>. Fetal Bladder). The orientation of the abdomen should place the stomach on the left and the liver on the right, with the portal vein extending away from the stomach. The umbilical cord insertion into the anterior abdominal wall is evaluated. Notably, the physiologic bowel herniation into the umbilical cord base is seen before 13 weeks and is not associated with anomalies. The bladder should contain fluid. If the kidneys are not visualized in the coronal view, renal artery power or color Doppler imaging is recommended.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129476.r29">[29]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129476.r13">[13]</xref></p>
        <p>Potential abnormalities that may be identified include cystic collections within the abdomen or an enlarged bladder greater than 7 mm, which could indicate aneuploidy or urinary tract obstruction.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129476.r13">[13]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129476.r30">[30]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Spine and Extremities</bold>
</p>
        <p>Three long bones should be identified in each extremity. Feet and hands should be documented. A detailed assessment of fingers and toes is required if an abnormality is suspected. The spine is evaluated in the longitudinal and axial planes with attention to scoliosis or irregularity. If skeletal dysplasia is suspected, the calvarial shape, thorax, ribs, and scapula should be evaluated.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129476.r31">[31]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129476.r13">[13]</xref>&#x000a0;Limitations of this evaluation include that the distal spine is not completely ossified at this gestational period.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129476.r13">[13]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Placenta, Uterus, and Adnexa</bold>
</p>
        <p>The placental position relative to the lower uterine segment, umbilical cord insertion, and echotexture must be evaluated. If PAS is suspected, a transvaginal approach with a partially full bladder should be performed with color Doppler and myometrial thinning, bladder wall interface; uterine vesicular vascularity abnormalities should be documented. The size, location, and number of leiomyomata should be documented. The ovaries, adnexa, and cul-de-sac should be visualized. Any Mullerian duct anomalies should be documented.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129476.r13">[13]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129476.r32">[32]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129476.r33">[33]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129476.r34">[34]</xref></p>
        <p>The components of a standard second-trimester anatomy examination and a detailed second-trimester anatomy examination are summarized in the tables at the end of this article. A detailed, comprehensive anatomy scan includes all of the components of the standard anatomy scan, with additional specific components guided by the initial indication for the scan. Also, specific ultrasound findings during the standard anatomy scan can direct additional detailed anatomic evaluation as determined by the reading physician. Therefore, not all of the components listed in the detailed description may be indicated in every detailed anatomic scan. Clinical judgment is warranted.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129476.r14">[14]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-129476.s10" sec-type="Complications">
        <title>Complications</title>
        <p>No major risks or complications to the fetus or mother undergoing a transabdominal ultrasound have been identified. A potential complication in the transvaginal approach is the possibility of inadvertently inserting the probe through the cervical os, causing a hemorrhage in the setting of an unknown placenta previa or vasa previa. However, this risk is very low if the scan clinician or sonographer is properly credentialed and trained in the technique. Taking precautions by starting&#x000a0;each examination with the transabdominal approach to&#x000a0;locate the placenta can avoid this possible risk.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-129476.s11" sec-type="Clinical Significance">
        <title>Clinical Significance</title>
        <p>Screening for fetal anomalies is performed in the first and second trimesters using NT screening, maternal serum cell-free DNA testing, or serum quad screen testing. When the screening test results are abnormal or suspicious for aneuploidy, diagnostic karyotyping is offered, along with a detailed anatomic ultrasound to evaluate for structural anomalies, which can be performed in the late first trimester. Findings of cystic hygroma, short femur, coarctation of the aorta, hypoplastic left heart, renal anomalies, cardiac defects, and abdominal wall hernias are highly suspicious for aneuploidy, especially when combined, and should prompt further diagnostic testing.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129476.r3">[3]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129476.r4">[4]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129476.r5">[5]</xref></p>
        <p>Diagnostic testing is outside the scope of this topic but includes chorionic villus sampling, performed between 10&#x000a0;and 13 weeks of gestation, and amniocentesis, which is optimally performed between 15 and 20 weeks of gestation. Depending on the state law, if a genetic abnormality incompatible with life is diagnosed before the third trimester, patients may have more reproductive options. Diagnosing major fetal abnormalities that will require immediate postnatal surgical intervention decreases morbidity and mortality when an interprofessional approach is taken to facilitate proper care and resources.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129476.r4">[4]</xref></p>
        <p>Abnormalities in screening tests are not always associated with aneuploidy but can be markers of adverse pregnancy outcomes. For example, enlarged NT is associated with miscarriage, intrauterine death, or congenital heart defects. The risk of adverse outcomes is proportional to the degree of NT enlargement. An increased thickness above 3.0 mm (or above the 99th percentile for crown-rump length) is directly proportional to an increased risk for aneuploidy (Down syndrome and Turner syndrome) and structural abnormalities unrelated to aneuploidy. Congenital heart disease is the most commonly&#x000a0;associated malformation in fetuses with euploid karyotypes. Genetic counseling, invasive genetic testing, a detailed fetal anatomy scan, and a fetal echocardiogram&#x000a0;are recommended, and they should be cautiously monitored throughout the antenatal period.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129476.r35">[35]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129476.r36">[36]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-129476.s12" sec-type="Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes ">
        <title>Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes </title>
        <p>Fetal ultrasonography plays a central role in evaluating fetal development and detecting anomalies throughout pregnancy, and its importance is increasing in high-risk populations. Early identification of maternal risk factors or exposures during the initial prenatal visit guides appropriate timing and selection of standardized ultrasound examinations, including standard, specialized, and limited studies. Advances in imaging enable the detection of many structural abnormalities as early as the late first trimester, allowing the timely diagnosis of conditions such as neural tube defects, cardiac anomalies, and placental disorders. Accurate interpretation and adherence to guideline-based protocols improve risk stratification, facilitate early intervention, and reduce morbidity and mortality by enabling appropriate surveillance and planning for delivery.</p>
        <p>Interprofessional collaboration is essential to optimize outcomes. Obstetricians, primary care clinicians, and advanced practitioners identify risk factors and coordinate care, while ultrasonographers acquire high-quality images and communicate findings promptly. Maternal-fetal medicine specialists provide advanced interpretation and guide management for high-risk cases. Nurses support patient education, monitoring, and follow-up, while pharmacists contribute to medication safety in complex pregnancies. Coordinated communication, timely referral to tertiary centers, and shared decision-making ensure appropriate diagnostic evaluation, risk reduction, and prevention of complications, ultimately improving patient-centered care and safety.</p>
      </sec>
